course: dictionary
lesson: 4944
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The dictionary gives them as numbered senses, and the context tells you which one is meant. The word impressionist is a good example: it can name a performer, a painter, or a style.
explain: «Shadows usually have colour in them, and outdoor shadows, such as on snow, often look blue or purple; the French Impressionists of the 1870s became famous for painting blue and purple shadows.» Here the words «French», «1870s» and «painting» tell you that an impressionist is a painter, not a performer.
explain: When you meet a word with several meanings, read the sentence around it and ask: which meaning fits here? Then try that meaning in place of the word. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right one.
fact: Impressionists were a group of painters in France in the late 1800s.
fact: Claude Monet and Pierre-Auguste Renoir were two famous Impressionist painters.
fact: Impressionist painters used short brushstrokes and dabs of colour to show light and a moment as it looked.
fact: An impressionist can also be a performer who copies the voices and ways of famous people for fun.
fact: A word with more than one meaning is called a word with several senses, and context helps you choose the right one.
exercise: «Shadows usually have colour in them, and outdoor shadows, such as on snow, often look blue or purple.» What does this sentence say about shadows on snow? | Always black | *Often blue or purple | Bright yellow | Only seen on sunny days
exercise: A TV impressionist does funny celebrity voices. What is an impressionist in this sense? | *A performer who copies famous people | A painter who lived in France | A person who forgets their lines | A camera operator on a TV set
exercise: Which description fits Impressionist paintings? | Printed in black ink only | Carved from stone | Written in long, rhyming lines | *Quick dabs of colour that show a moment
test: «The French Impressionists of the 1870s became famous for painting blue and purple shadows.» Who were the French Impressionists? | Famous footballers from Paris | Scientists who studied shadows | Cooks in a busy café | *Painters who showed light and colour in quick brushstrokes
test: A gallery sign says, «Impressionist Room». What does Impressionist mean here? | A room that is always dark | A room for writing poems in French | *In the style of the Impressionist painters | A room for performers and TV impressionists
exam: Which sentence uses impressionist to mean a performer? | «The Impressionist room was full of water lilies and gardens.» | «The shadows on the snow looked blue and purple.» | «The sky was painted in an Impressionist style, with soft dabs of colour.» | *«The impressionist on TV copied the prime minister's voice perfectly.»
